    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d489">

                <pb facs="0604" n="594" sameAs="frus:frus1942China/pg594"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24/1334: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-05-22T18:00:00+04:00">May 22, 1942—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 22—4:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>432. Department’s 217, May 9, 8 [<hi rendition="#italic">7</hi>] p.m. I
                    endeavored to impress upon Vyshinski<anchor n="4" xml:id="d489-f1"/> yesterday
                    the hope of my Government that the Soviet Government would do everything
                    possible to facilitate the movement of supplies to China through the Soviet
                    Union. Vyshinski stated that he was not fully acquainted with all the details of
                    the problem but would willingly inform the interested Soviet authorities of the
                    concern of the American Government in the matter.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Standley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d489-f1" n="4" xml:id="d489-f1-content">
                <p>Soviet Assistant Commissar for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
